  "notes": "Themes searched: world, society, culture and globalism (nation, Israel, colonialism, race, assimilation, refugees, withdrawal, anti-communism), plus economy, government, liberty, ecology, technology and means. The sources record no position of their own there, and the general Marxist-Leninist or Soviet-state features of the old audit (planning, public ownership, party rule, welfare, unions) are null here because they are not stated about Zionism. Scholars (s2) report that much campaign writing, including authors the state published, drew on the Protocols; the official line denied this. The Great Soviet Encyclopedia entry on Zionism and the UN debate texts were not retrievable, so they are not cited. The Anti-Zionist Committee manifesto is known here through a news report.\n\nMerged with an independent claude-sonnet-5-5+GPT-6.1-Sol audit: 6 answers taken from it, 5 agreed, 2 conflicts settled by the integrator.\n\nclaude-sonnet-5-5+GPT-6.1-Sol notes: Independent re-audit from sources; earlier answers were not consulted. Searched the downloaded primary texts by theme, including economy (property, credit, wages), government (elections, courts, parliament), world (war, territorial withdrawal, refugees, UN, nuclear weapons), liberty (emigration and repression), culture (Hebrew, assimilation, identity), society, ecology and technology. Economic descriptions of alleged Zionist finance are not a domestic economic programme; the sources provide no distinct environmental or technology policy. Statements about all diasporas, all indigenous peoples or private self-defence are not answered as stated positions from one regional case. The original source’s allegations are reported as its claims; critics’ assessments are used to document campaign practice and context, not substituted for the doctrine’s own positions. Final evidence check leaves nearby-case extrapolations unknown where no stated general principle establishes the broader claim.",
  "keyFigures": [
    {
